   "lane": "raw",
   "answer": "Between `requests.get()` and the bytes hitting the socket, several key classes hand off the request through the lifecycle:\n\n1. **`Session`** (in `src/requests/sessions.py`):\n   - The top-level `requests.get()` calls `requests.api.get()`, which creates or uses a `Session` object (`src/requests/api.py`).\n   - The `Session` constructs a **`Request`** object, prepares it into a **`PreparedRequest`** object, and handles session-level features like cookies and hooks (`src/requests/sessions.py`).\n\n2. **`HTTPAdapter`** (in `src/requests/adapters.py`):\n   - The `Session` delegates the actual sending of the `PreparedRequest` to an **`HTTPAdapter`** via `adapter.send(request, ...)`.\n   - The `HTTPAdapter` resolves connection pools, sets up TLS/certificates, populates headers, and determines whether the request is chunked.\n\n3. **urllib3 Connection / Pool Classes** (under the hood via `HTTPAdapter`):\n   - The `HTTPAdapter` obtains a connection object (e.g., urllib3's `HTTPConnectionPool` / `HTTPSConnectionPool` and their underlying connection instances) and calls `conn.urlopen(...)`.\n   - Ultimately, urllib3 formats the HTTP request and writes the raw bytes directly to the underlying Python socket.",
